      "id": "writing-08",
      "title": "The Start-up That Closed Before It Opened",
      "category": "Entrepreneurship satire",
      "summary": "A hostel idea grows into a pitch before its team investigates the problem.",
      "paragraphs": [
        "The idea began with laundry. Someone had forgotten to collect it; someone else said there should be an app. By the time the clothes were dry, the app had gained food ordering, club events, lost-and-found, a chatbot and a name that sounded like a satellite.",
        "“We should validate the problem,” said one student.",
        "“Obviously,” said the others. They then spent forty minutes choosing a font for the pitch deck.",
        "At 2 a.m., roles were distributed. Founder. Co-founder. Product lead. One person became “strategy” because they had a charged laptop. The finance discussion lasted until somebody asked where the money would come from. “We’ll figure that out after traction,” said the founder. At present, traction consisted of three people nodding in a hostel room.",
        "A shared document appeared: Campus Platform Vision. It had a logo and an empty section headed “User Research.” The group planned to speak to students in the morning. In the morning, one member had a lab, another had a quiz, and the founder slept through an alarm. The document acquired no new text, but its title was changed twice. In one version the word platform became super-app, which was apparently progress.",
        "I like this kind of midnight ambition, even when it is ridiculous. Students notice small irritations that people running large systems stop noticing. The trouble starts when the team falls in love with its solution before it knows whether anyone has the problem. Perhaps the laundry itself is fine and the real issue is the one machine everyone wants at the same hour. Perhaps no app is needed; perhaps a whiteboard would do.",
        "The group could have asked five people the next day. One question each. No logo, no funding plan, no “ecosystem.” If the answers were interesting, they could make something small and ugly and see whether anyone used it.",
        "Months later, the shared document is still there. The founder finds it while looking for lab notes. They laugh, then message the old group: “Laundry thing?” Two people react with 👍. The third asks, “Can we first find out if anybody wants it?” For once, the venture may have a beginning."
      ],
