      "title": "The Voice Note You Do Not Send",
      "category": "Friendship and reflection",
      "summary": "An unsent voice note explores the difficulty of asking friends for support.",
      "paragraphs": [
        "“Are you okay?”",
        "You type “yes” and delete it. “Just tired” is closer, but it makes the day sound as though a nap will sort it out. You hold the microphone button and talk for almost a minute: the deadline you missed, how embarrassed you are about it, how everyone else seems to have their act together. You play it back once. You sound unlike yourself. The note stays unsent.",
        "There are good reasons to keep a thought private. There is also a less noble one: we want a friend to know without having to tell them. It is an understandable wish and a remarkably unreliable communication method.",
        "A hostel can be noisy enough to hide a quiet person. Friends know which class you skipped, what you ordered, and the approximate state of your project. They may not know that a small failure has been following you around for days. You have helped them miss it by saying “all good” with professional speed.",
        "Later, you send something shorter: “Today was bad. Free for a walk?” It does not carry the whole story. It opens a door. Your friend replies after a while: “Ten minutes. I’m finishing this thing.” The walk lasts twenty-five. Neither of you finds a grand answer. Your friend tells you about a presentation they once ruined by opening the wrong file. The first slide was a grocery list. You laugh. The deadline is still missed when you return, but it is no longer the only thing in the room.",
        "Sometimes a friend will be busy. Sometimes the person you choose will not know what to say. That does not make the attempt a mistake. It may mean trying another time, or asking somebody better equipped to help if the problem is larger than a walk can hold.",
        "The minute-long recording is still on your phone. You could delete it. You could also keep it as a reminder that the sentence you needed was much simpler than the speech you rehearsed: “Can you come out for a bit?”"
      ],
